select public.fd_ai_upsert_chunks('fd-textbook', '1b1a21163698', 14, $aim_json$[{"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:14.0#1", "source_key": "fd-textbook:14.0", "chapter": 14, "section": "14.0", "heading": "Overview", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 1, "title": "Chapter 14, The Greeks and Dynamic Hedging — 14.0 Overview", "body": "Black, F. (1976). The pricing of commodity contracts. Journal of Financial Economics, 3(1–2), 167–179.\nGarman, M. B., and Kohlhagen, S. W. (1983). Foreign currency option values. Journal of International Money and Finance, 2(3), 231–237.\nHull, J. C. (2022). Options, Futures, and Other Derivatives (11th ed.). Pearson. Chapters 17–18.\nLeland, H. E., and Rubinstein, M. (1988). The evolution of portfolio insurance. In D. L. Luskin (Ed.), Portfolio Insurance: A Guide to Dynamic Hedging. Wiley.\nMerton, R. C. (1973). Theory of rational option pricing. Bell Journal of Economics and Management Science, 4(1), 141–183."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:14.0#2", "source_key": "fd-textbook:14.0", "chapter": 14, "section": "14.0", "heading": "Decide first", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 2, "title": "Chapter 14, The Greeks and Dynamic Hedging — 14.0 Decide first", "body": "⚙ Decision — choose first; the theory follows\nHow should a dealer hedge an option that it has sold?\nA dealer has sold to a client European calls on 100,000 Northwind shares, with a strike of $30 and three months to expiration, for $170,000. The share price is $28. The Black–Scholes–Merton value of the calls, at a volatility of 39 percent and a risk-free rate of 5 percent, is $151,700. The dealer wishes to retain the difference of $18,300 whatever happens to the share price. Which course of action serves that purpose?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:14.1#3", "source_key": "fd-textbook:14.1", "chapter": 14, "section": "14.1", "heading": "The position of an option writer", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 3, "title": "Chapter 14, The Greeks and Dynamic Hedging — 14.1 The position of an option writer", "body": "A financial institution that sells an option to a client in the over-the-counter market has acquired a risk. If an identical option is traded on an exchange, the institution can buy it and the risk is removed. More often the option has been tailored to the client and has no traded counterpart. The institution must then manage the risk by other means. This chapter describes those means. The dealer of the opening decision serves as the example throughout.\nTwo simple policies are available, and both are unsatisfactory.\nA naked position consists in doing nothing. If the share price is below $30 in three months, the calls are not exercised and the dealer keeps the $170,000. If the price is $40, the dealer must deliver shares worth $4,000,000 for $3,000,000, a cost of $1,000,000 that greatly exceeds the premium.\nA covered position consists in buying 100,000 shares at once, for $2,800,000. If the calls are exercised, the shares are delivered and the dealer has done well. If the price falls to $20, the calls expire unexercised and the dealer has lost $800,000 on the shares."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:14.1#4", "source_key": "fd-textbook:14.1", "chapter": 14, "section": "14.1", "heading": "The position of an option writer", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 4, "title": "Chapter 14, The Greeks and Dynamic Hedging — 14.1 The position of an option writer", "body": "Neither policy secures the profit of $18,300. By put–call parity the covered position is equivalent to a written put, so that the dealer has merely exchanged an exposure to rising prices for an exposure to falling prices. A satisfactory hedge must ensure that the cost of meeting the obligation is close to $151,700 in every case."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:14.1#5", "source_key": "fd-textbook:14.1", "chapter": 14, "section": "14.1", "heading": "The position of an option writer", "kind": "warning", "ord": 5, "title": "Chapter 14, The Greeks and Dynamic Hedging — 14.1 The position of an option writer", "body": "Common error\nIt is tempting to hold the shares only when they are needed: buy 100,000 shares whenever the price rises above $30 and sell them whenever it falls below. This stop-loss strategy appears to ensure that the dealer holds the shares in exactly those cases in which the calls are exercised, and at no cost, since every purchase and every sale is made at $30. It fails for two reasons. The purchases are made at a price slightly above $30 and the sales at a price slightly below, and the price may cross $30 many times. And the cash flows occur at different dates and cannot be added without discounting. Simulation shows that the cost of the strategy is highly variable and on average exceeds the value of the option."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:14.2#6", "source_key": "fd-textbook:14.2", "chapter": 14, "section": "14.2", "heading": "Delta", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 6, "title": "Chapter 14, The Greeks and Dynamic Hedging — 14.2 Delta", "body": "The delta of an option, \\(\\Delta\\), is the rate of change of its price with respect to the price of the underlying asset: \\[ \\Delta=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial S}. \\] It is the slope of the curve that relates the price of the option to the price of the underlying, and it is the quantity \\(\\Delta\\) of Chapter 10, the number of shares in the portfolio that replicates the option. A delta of 0.42 means that when the share price changes by a small amount, the price of the option changes by about 42 percent of that amount.\nResult 14.1 — Delta of European options\nFor European options on a share that pays no dividend, \\[ \\Delta_{\\text{call}}=N(d_1), \\qquad \\Delta_{\\text{put}}=N(d_1)-1, \\] where \\(d_1\\) is defined in Result 12.2. The delta of a call lies between 0 and 1, and the delta of a put between \\(-1\\) and 0. For an asset that provides income at the rate \\(q\\), both expressions are multiplied by \\(e^{-qT}\\)."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:14.2#7", "source_key": "fd-textbook:14.2", "chapter": 14, "section": "14.2", "heading": "Delta", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 7, "title": "Chapter 14, The Greeks and Dynamic Hedging — 14.2 Delta", "body": "The delta of a call is close to zero when the option is far out of the money, close to one when it is far in the money, and near one half when the share price is near the strike. The two deltas differ by exactly one, as put–call parity requires: a long call and a short put with the same strike together behave as one share."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:14.2#8", "source_key": "fd-textbook:14.2", "chapter": 14, "section": "14.2", "heading": "Key terms", "kind": "term", "ord": 8, "title": "Chapter 14, The Greeks and Dynamic Hedging — 14.2 Key terms", "body": "Key terms: Delta of a portfolio: the sum of the deltas of its components, each multiplied by the quantity held; a share has a delta of 1, and a short position has the negative of the delta of the corresponding long position. Delta-neutral position: a position with a delta of zero, whose value does not change, to a first approximation, when the price of the underlying changes by a small amount. Delta hedging: the maintenance of a delta-neutral position by trading the underlying asset. Rebalancing: the adjustment of the hedge as delta changes."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:14.2#9", "source_key": "fd-textbook:14.2", "chapter": 14, "section": "14.2", "heading": "Delta", "kind": "example", "ord": 9, "title": "Chapter 14, The Greeks and Dynamic Hedging — 14.2 Delta", "body": "Worked example 14.1 — The delta hedge of the dealer\nFind the delta of the calls that the dealer has written and the holding of shares that makes the position delta neutral.\n1\nDelta of one call. From Worked example 12.1, \\(d_1=-0.1922\\), so that \\(\\Delta=N(-0.1922)=0.4238\\).\n2\nDelta of the written calls. The dealer is short 100,000 calls: \\(-100{,}000\\times0.4238=-42{,}380\\). The position behaves as a short position in 42,380 shares.\n3\nThe hedge. The dealer buys 42,380 shares, at a cost of \\(42{,}380\\times28=\\$1{,}186{,}640\\). The delta of the whole position is \\(-42{,}380+42{,}380=0\\).\n4\nVerification. If the share price rises by $0.10, the shares gain \\(42{,}380\\times0.10=\\$4{,}238\\) and the calls rise in value by about \\(100{,}000\\times0.4238\\times0.10=\\$4{,}238\\), which is a loss to the dealer. The two effects offset."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:14.3#10", "source_key": "fd-textbook:14.3", "chapter": 14, "section": "14.3", "heading": "Dynamic delta hedging", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 10, "title": "Chapter 14, The Greeks and Dynamic Hedging — 14.3 Dynamic delta hedging", "body": "The delta of an option does not remain constant. If the Northwind share price rises to $30 during the first week, the delta of the call rises to 0.562, and the dealer must buy a further 13,800 shares to remain delta neutral. If the price falls to $26, delta falls to 0.271 and 15,200 shares must be sold. The hedge is therefore adjusted repeatedly through the life of the option. A hedge of this kind is called a dynamic hedge, in contrast with a static hedge, which is established once and left in place.\nThe procedure has a feature that explains its cost. Shares are bought after the price has risen and sold after it has fallen. Each round of buying high and selling low loses a small amount, and the accumulated losses are the cost of the hedge. The central result of Chapters 10 and 12 may be restated in these terms: if the volatility of the share is 39 percent throughout and the hedge is adjusted continuously, the present value of the cost of the hedge is exactly the Black–Scholes–Merton value, $151,700, whatever path the share price follows. The dealer who sold the calls for $170,000 is left with $18,300."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:14.3#11", "source_key": "fd-textbook:14.3", "chapter": 14, "section": "14.3", "heading": "Dynamic delta hedging", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 11, "title": "Chapter 14, The Greeks and Dynamic Hedging — 14.3 Dynamic delta hedging", "body": "In practice the hedge is adjusted at intervals, and the cost varies from one path to another. Interactive figure 14.1 carries out the hedge along a simulated path and then repeats it over many paths to show how the variation depends on the frequency of adjustment."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:14.3#12", "source_key": "fd-textbook:14.3", "chapter": 14, "section": "14.3", "heading": "Dynamic delta hedging", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 12, "title": "Chapter 14, The Greeks and Dynamic Hedging — 14.3 Dynamic delta hedging", "body": "⚙ Interactive figure 14.1 — Delta hedging along a path\nThe cost of hedging 100,000 written calls\nThe dealer holds \\(100{,}000\\times\\Delta\\) shares, with \\(\\Delta\\) computed at a volatility of 39 percent, and finances the holding by borrowing at 5 percent. Choose the frequency of adjustment and the volatility that the share in fact exhibits, and generate new paths. The table records the hedge at the end of each week. The cost of the hedge is the amount borrowed at expiration, less the $3,000,000 received if the calls are exercised, discounted to the present."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:14.3#13", "source_key": "fd-textbook:14.3", "chapter": 14, "section": "14.3", "heading": "Dynamic delta hedging", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 13, "title": "Chapter 14, The Greeks and Dynamic Hedging — 14.3 Dynamic delta hedging", "body": "Three observations should be drawn from the figure. First, when the realized volatility is 39 percent, the average cost of the hedge is close to $151,700 at every frequency of adjustment: the frequency affects the dispersion of the cost and not its average. Second, the dispersion falls as the hedge is adjusted more often, approximately in proportion to the square root of the interval between adjustments. Third, when the share proves more volatile than the dealer assumed, the hedge costs more than the value at which the calls were priced, and when it proves less volatile the hedge costs less. A dealer who has sold an option and hedged its delta is no longer exposed to the direction of the share price. The dealer remains exposed to its volatility.\nConcept check 14.1\nA trader has written 500 put options, each on one share, with a delta of −0.30. What position in the share makes the trader delta neutral?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:14.4#14", "source_key": "fd-textbook:14.4", "chapter": 14, "section": "14.4", "heading": "Theta", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 14, "title": "Chapter 14, The Greeks and Dynamic Hedging — 14.4 Theta", "body": "The theta of an option, \\(\\Theta\\), is the rate of change of its price with respect to the passage of time, all else remaining the same. It is also called the time decay of the option. The formulas are in Table 14.1. Theta is computed with time measured in years; it is usually quoted per day, by dividing by 365 for the decay per calendar day or by 252 for the decay per trading day.\nThe theta of an option held is usually negative: as expiration approaches and nothing else changes, the option loses value. For the Northwind call, \\(\\Theta=-4.794\\) per year, or \\(-4.794/365=-\\$0.0131\\) per calendar day. The 100,000 calls lose about $1,310 of value on each day on which the share price does not move, and this is a gain to the dealer who wrote them. Theta is greatest in magnitude for options that are at the money and close to expiration.\nTheta differs in kind from delta. The future price of the share is uncertain; the passage of time is not. It makes sense to hedge against changes in the share price and none to hedge against the passage of time. Theta is nevertheless of interest, because of its relation to gamma, which Section 14.6 states."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:14.5#15", "source_key": "fd-textbook:14.5", "chapter": 14, "section": "14.5", "heading": "Gamma", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 15, "title": "Chapter 14, The Greeks and Dynamic Hedging — 14.5 Gamma", "body": "The gamma of an option, \\(\\Gamma\\), is the rate of change of its delta with respect to the price of the underlying asset: \\[ \\Gamma=\\frac{\\partial^{2}f}{\\partial S^{2}}. \\] It measures the curvature of the relation between the price of the option and the price of the underlying. If gamma is small, delta changes slowly, and a delta-neutral position needs adjustment only occasionally. If gamma is large, delta changes rapidly, and a hedge that is left unadjusted soon ceases to be a hedge.\nFor a European call or put on a share that pays no dividend, \\[ \\Gamma=\\frac{N'(d_1)}{S_0\\sigma\\sqrt{T}}, \\qquad N'(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}}e^{-x^{2}/2}, \\] where \\(N'(x)\\) is the standard normal density. The gamma of a call equals the gamma of the put with the same strike and expiration. It is positive for an option held and negative for an option written. For the Northwind call, \\(N'(-0.1922)=0.3916\\) and \\(\\Gamma=0.3916/(28\\times0.39\\times0.5)=0.0717\\): when the share price rises by $1, the delta of the call rises by about 0.072."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:14.5#16", "source_key": "fd-textbook:14.5", "chapter": 14, "section": "14.5", "heading": "Gamma", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 16, "title": "Chapter 14, The Greeks and Dynamic Hedging — 14.5 Gamma", "body": "Gamma accounts for the error of a delta hedge when the price moves by more than a small amount. Expanding the value of a position \\(\\Pi\\) to the second order in the change \\(\\Delta S\\) of the share price over a short interval,\nResult 14.2 — The delta–gamma approximation\n\\[ \\Delta\\Pi\\approx\\Delta\\times\\Delta S+\\tfrac12\\Gamma(\\Delta S)^{2}+\\Theta\\,\\Delta t . \\] For a delta-neutral position the first term is zero, and the change in value over an interval in which the price moves by \\(\\Delta S\\) is approximately \\(\\tfrac12\\Gamma(\\Delta S)^{2}+\\Theta\\,\\Delta t\\)."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:14.5#17", "source_key": "fd-textbook:14.5", "chapter": 14, "section": "14.5", "heading": "Gamma", "kind": "example", "ord": 17, "title": "Chapter 14, The Greeks and Dynamic Hedging — 14.5 Gamma", "body": "Worked example 14.2 — The error of the delta hedge\nThe dealer is short 100,000 calls and long 42,380 shares. The share price moves at once from $28 to $30. Find the change in the value of the position, and compare it with the delta–gamma approximation. Repeat for a fall to $26.\n1\nExact change, rise to $30. The value of a call rises from $1.5173 to $2.5067, a change of $0.9895. The shares gain \\(0.4238\\times2=\\$0.8476\\) per call hedged. The dealer loses \\(100{,}000\\times(0.9895-0.8476)=\\$14{,}190\\).\n2\nApproximation. The position is short 100,000 calls, so its gamma is \\(-100{,}000\\times0.0717=-7{,}173\\). By Result 14.2, \\(\\Delta\\Pi\\approx\\tfrac12\\times(-7{,}173)\\times2^{2}=-\\$14{,}346\\).\n3\nFall to $26. The value of a call falls by $0.7057, and the shares lose $0.8476 per call hedged. The dealer loses \\(100{,}000\\times(0.8476-0.7057)=\\$14{,}190\\). The approximation is again −$14,346.\n4\nInterpretation. A position with negative gamma loses when the price moves in either direction, and the loss increases with the square of the move. Delta hedging removes the exposure to the direction of the price and leaves an exposure to the size of its movements."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:14.5#18", "source_key": "fd-textbook:14.5", "chapter": 14, "section": "14.5", "heading": "Gamma", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 18, "title": "Chapter 14, The Greeks and Dynamic Hedging — 14.5 Gamma", "body": "The sign of gamma thus divides option positions into two classes. A position that is long gamma, such as a purchased call, a purchased put, or the straddle of Section 9.4, gains from large movements once its delta has been hedged, and pays for this through time decay. A position that is short gamma, such as that of the dealer, collects time decay and loses from large movements."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:14.6#19", "source_key": "fd-textbook:14.6", "chapter": 14, "section": "14.6", "heading": "The relation between delta, theta, and gamma", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 19, "title": "Chapter 14, The Greeks and Dynamic Hedging — 14.6 The relation between delta, theta, and gamma", "body": "The Black–Scholes–Merton differential equation, Result 12.1, holds for every derivative on a share that pays no dividend, and therefore for every portfolio \\(\\Pi\\) of such derivatives. Since \\(\\partial\\Pi/\\partial t=\\Theta\\), \\(\\partial\\Pi/\\partial S=\\Delta\\), and \\(\\partial^{2}\\Pi/\\partial S^{2}=\\Gamma\\), the equation states the following.\nResult 14.3 — Delta, theta, and gamma\n\\[ \\Theta+rS\\Delta+\\tfrac12\\sigma^{2}S^{2}\\Gamma=r\\Pi . \\] For a delta-neutral portfolio, \\(\\Theta+\\tfrac12\\sigma^{2}S^{2}\\Gamma=r\\Pi\\).\nFor the Northwind call the three terms are \\(-4.7942\\), \\(0.05\\times28\\times0.4238=0.5933\\), and \\(\\tfrac12\\times0.1521\\times784\\times0.07173=4.2768\\). Their sum is 0.0759, which equals \\(0.05\\times1.5173\\), as the result requires.\nThe result shows that theta and gamma are two aspects of one property. In a delta-neutral portfolio whose value is small, a large positive gamma is accompanied by a large negative theta, and conversely. The holder of options pays time decay each day and receives in return the gains of Worked example 14.2 when the price moves. The price of an option is such that, when the volatility is as assumed, the two are equal on average."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:14.7#20", "source_key": "fd-textbook:14.7", "chapter": 14, "section": "14.7", "heading": "Vega and rho", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 20, "title": "Chapter 14, The Greeks and Dynamic Hedging — 14.7 Vega and rho", "body": "The Black–Scholes–Merton model assumes that volatility is constant. In practice the implied volatilities at which options trade change from day to day, and the value of an option position changes with them. The vega of an option, \\(\\mathcal{V}\\), is the rate of change of its price with respect to the volatility of the underlying asset. For a European call or put on a share that pays no dividend, \\[ \\mathcal{V}=S_0\\sqrt{T}\\,N'(d_1). \\] Vega is the same for a call and for the put with the same strike and expiration, is positive for an option held, and is greatest for options that are at the money. Unlike gamma, it increases with the time to expiration. For the Northwind call, \\(\\mathcal{V}=28\\times0.5\\times0.3916=5.483\\). The formula measures volatility as a decimal, so that an increase of one percentage point, from 39 to 40 percent, raises the price of the call by about \\(5.483\\times0.01=\\$0.055\\). The dealer, who is short 100,000 calls, loses about $5,480 for each percentage point by which implied volatility rises."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:14.7#21", "source_key": "fd-textbook:14.7", "chapter": 14, "section": "14.7", "heading": "Vega and rho", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 21, "title": "Chapter 14, The Greeks and Dynamic Hedging — 14.7 Vega and rho", "body": "There is an inconsistency in computing vega from a model that assumes volatility to be constant. Practitioners accept it: the vega computed from the Black–Scholes–Merton formula has been found to be close to the vega computed from models in which volatility is itself uncertain.\nThe rho of an option is the rate of change of its price with respect to the risk-free rate. For a European call on a share that pays no dividend it is \\(KTe^{-rT}N(d_2)\\), which is positive; for a put it is \\(-KTe^{-rT}N(-d_2)\\), which is negative. For the Northwind call, rho is 2.587, so that an increase of one percentage point in the rate raises the price by about $0.026. For options on shares with a life of a few months, rho is the least important of the sensitivities.\nTable 14.1 Sensitivities of European options on an asset that provides income at the rate \\(q\\)\nMeasureDefinitionCallPut\nDelta\\(\\partial f/\\partial S\\)\\(e^{-qT}N(d_1)\\)\\(e^{-qT}[N(d_1)-1]\\)\nGamma\\(\\partial^{2}f/\\partial S^{2}\\)\\(\\dfrac{N'(d_1)e^{-qT}}{S_0\\sigma\\sqrt{T}}\\)"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:14.7#22", "source_key": "fd-textbook:14.7", "chapter": 14, "section": "14.7", "heading": "Vega and rho", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 22, "title": "Chapter 14, The Greeks and Dynamic Hedging — 14.7 Vega and rho", "body": "Theta\\(\\partial f/\\partial t\\)\\(-\\dfrac{S_0N'(d_1)\\sigma e^{-qT}}{2\\sqrt{T}}+qS_0N(d_1)e^{-qT}-rKe^{-rT}N(d_2)\\)\\(-\\dfrac{S_0N'(d_1)\\sigma e^{-qT}}{2\\sqrt{T}}-qS_0N(-d_1)e^{-qT}+rKe^{-rT}N(-d_2)\\)\nVega\\(\\partial f/\\partial\\sigma\\)\\(S_0\\sqrt{T}\\,N'(d_1)e^{-qT}\\)\nRho\\(\\partial f/\\partial r\\)\\(KTe^{-rT}N(d_2)\\)\\(-KTe^{-rT}N(-d_2)\\)\nIn Table 14.1, \\(d_1\\) and \\(d_2\\) are those of Result 13.1. Setting \\(q=0\\) gives the sensitivities of options on a share that pays no dividend; \\(q\\) is the dividend yield for an index, the foreign interest rate for a currency, and \\(r\\) for a futures contract, as in Chapter 13. The sensitivities of the Northwind options are collected in Table 14.2.\nTable 14.2 Sensitivities of the three-month Northwind options with a strike of $30 (share price $28, volatility 39 percent, risk-free rate 5 percent)\nMeasureCallPutMeaning for one call\nPrice$1.5173$3.1446\nDelta0.4238−0.5762Gains $0.042 when the share price rises by $0.10\nGamma0.07170.0717Delta rises by 0.072 when the share price rises by $1\nTheta (per year)−4.794−3.313Loses $0.0131 per calendar day\nVega5.4835.483Gains $0.055 when volatility rises by one percentage point"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:14.7#23", "source_key": "fd-textbook:14.7", "chapter": 14, "section": "14.7", "heading": "Vega and rho", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 23, "title": "Chapter 14, The Greeks and Dynamic Hedging — 14.7 Vega and rho", "body": "Rho2.587−4.820Gains $0.026 when the rate rises by one percentage point"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:14.7#24", "source_key": "fd-textbook:14.7", "chapter": 14, "section": "14.7", "heading": "Vega and rho", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 24, "title": "Chapter 14, The Greeks and Dynamic Hedging — 14.7 Vega and rho", "body": "⚙ Interactive figure 14.2 — The sensitivities across share prices\nDelta, gamma, theta, and vega of options with a strike of $30\nSelect a sensitivity and vary the time to expiration and the volatility. The solid line refers to the call and the dashed line to the put; where the two coincide, one line is shown. Observe how each measure behaves as expiration approaches."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:14.7#25", "source_key": "fd-textbook:14.7", "chapter": 14, "section": "14.7", "heading": "Vega and rho", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 25, "title": "Chapter 14, The Greeks and Dynamic Hedging — 14.7 Vega and rho", "body": "⚙ Interactive figure 14.3 — Three-dimensional view\nA sensitivity as a function of the share price and the time to expiration\nThe surface shows the selected sensitivity of the call with a strike of $30, at a volatility of 39 percent, for share prices from $15 to $45 and from six months before expiration to one week before. The gold curve is the section at the selected time. Gamma and time decay concentrate at the strike as expiration approaches; vega diminishes; delta steepens toward a step. For theta the surface shows the daily loss of value, the negative of theta. Drag to rotate; scroll or pinch to zoom.\nTable equivalent of this figure"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:14.7#26", "source_key": "fd-textbook:14.7", "chapter": 14, "section": "14.7", "heading": "Vega and rho", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 26, "title": "Chapter 14, The Greeks and Dynamic Hedging — 14.7 Vega and rho", "body": "Concept check 14.2\nA delta-neutral position has a large negative gamma. Over the next day the share price does not move at all. What happens to the value of the position, and why?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:14.8#27", "source_key": "fd-textbook:14.8", "chapter": 14, "section": "14.8", "heading": "Making a portfolio gamma neutral and vega neutral", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 27, "title": "Chapter 14, The Greeks and Dynamic Hedging — 14.8 Making a portfolio gamma neutral and vega neutral", "body": "A position in the underlying asset has a delta of one and a gamma and a vega of zero: its price is a linear function of itself and does not depend on its volatility. Trading the asset can therefore change the delta of a portfolio and nothing else. The gamma and the vega of a portfolio can be changed only by trading instruments whose value is not linear in the price of the asset, which in practice means other options.\nSuppose that a delta-neutral portfolio has a gamma of \\(\\Gamma\\), and that a traded option has a gamma of \\(\\Gamma_T\\). Adding \\(w_T\\) of the traded options gives a gamma of \\(w_T\\Gamma_T+\\Gamma\\), which is zero when \\[ w_T=-\\frac{\\Gamma}{\\Gamma_T}. \\] The traded options have a delta of their own, so that the portfolio is no longer delta neutral, and the position in the underlying asset must then be adjusted. Vega is neutralized in the same way, with \\(w_T=-\\mathcal{V}/\\mathcal{V}_T\\). A single traded option cannot in general neutralize gamma and vega together. Two traded options are needed, and the quantities are found from two simultaneous equations."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:14.8#28", "source_key": "fd-textbook:14.8", "chapter": 14, "section": "14.8", "heading": "Making a portfolio gamma neutral and vega neutral", "kind": "example", "ord": 28, "title": "Chapter 14, The Greeks and Dynamic Hedging — 14.8 Making a portfolio gamma neutral and vega neutral", "body": "Worked example 14.3 — A portfolio neutral in delta, gamma, and vega\nAn options desk holds a portfolio of options on Northwind shares that is delta neutral, with a gamma of −6,000 and a vega of −6,000 per percentage point of volatility. Two options are traded: option A, a three-month call with a strike of $28 (delta 0.5642, gamma 0.0721, vega 0.0551 per percentage point), and option B, a six-month call with a strike of $30 (delta 0.4914, gamma 0.0517, vega 0.0790 per percentage point). Find the trades that make the portfolio neutral in all three respects.\n1\nThe two conditions. Let \\(w_A\\) and \\(w_B\\) be the numbers of the two options bought. Gamma: \\(0.0721w_A+0.0517w_B=6{,}000\\). Vega: \\(0.0551w_A+0.0790w_B=6{,}000\\).\n2\nSolution. \\(w_A=57{,}530\\) and \\(w_B=35{,}824\\). Options are bought, as expected, because the portfolio is short both gamma and vega.\n3\nRestoring delta neutrality. The options bought have a delta of \\(57{,}530\\times0.5642+35{,}824\\times0.4914=50{,}062\\). The desk sells 50,062 shares.\n4"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:14.8#29", "source_key": "fd-textbook:14.8", "chapter": 14, "section": "14.8", "heading": "Making a portfolio gamma neutral and vega neutral", "kind": "example", "ord": 29, "title": "Chapter 14, The Greeks and Dynamic Hedging — 14.8 Making a portfolio gamma neutral and vega neutral", "body": "Using option A alone. Gamma could be neutralized with \\(6{,}000/0.0721=83{,}218\\) of option A. The vega of the portfolio would then be \\(-6{,}000+83{,}218\\times0.0551=-1{,}415\\), which is not zero. One instrument cannot satisfy two conditions."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:14.8#30", "source_key": "fd-textbook:14.8", "chapter": 14, "section": "14.8", "heading": "Making a portfolio gamma neutral and vega neutral", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 30, "title": "Chapter 14, The Greeks and Dynamic Hedging — 14.8 Making a portfolio gamma neutral and vega neutral", "body": "The portfolio so constructed is neutral only at the present share price, volatility, and date. As these change, the sensitivities change, and the procedure must be repeated. In practice delta is restored to zero, or close to it, at least once a day, because the underlying asset can be traded at low cost. Gamma and vega are monitored and corrected only when they exceed limits set by the institution, because options are more costly to trade and a suitable option is not always available at a reasonable price."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:14.9#31", "source_key": "fd-textbook:14.9", "chapter": 14, "section": "14.9", "heading": "★ The profit and loss of a delta-hedged position", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 31, "title": "Chapter 14, The Greeks and Dynamic Hedging — 14.9 ★ The profit and loss of a delta-hedged position", "body": "Optional section. This section combines Results 14.2 and 14.3 to show what a delta-hedged option position gains and loses. It explains the third observation drawn from Interactive figure 14.1.\nConsider a position that is long one option and short \\(\\Delta\\) shares, financed at the risk-free rate, and suppose for simplicity that \\(r=0\\). The option was valued at a volatility \\(\\sigma_{\\text{imp}}\\). Over a short interval \\(\\Delta t\\) the share price changes by \\(\\Delta S\\). By Result 14.2 the position gains \\[ \\tfrac12\\Gamma(\\Delta S)^{2}+\\Theta\\,\\Delta t . \\] By Result 14.3 with \\(r=0\\), \\(\\Theta=-\\tfrac12\\sigma_{\\text{imp}}^{2}S^{2}\\Gamma\\). Substituting,\n\\[ \\text{gain over the interval}=\\tfrac12\\Gamma S^{2}\\left[\\left(\\frac{\\Delta S}{S}\\right)^{2}-\\sigma_{\\text{imp}}^{2}\\,\\Delta t\\right]. \\]"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:14.9#32", "source_key": "fd-textbook:14.9", "chapter": 14, "section": "14.9", "heading": "★ The profit and loss of a delta-hedged position", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 32, "title": "Chapter 14, The Greeks and Dynamic Hedging — 14.9 ★ The profit and loss of a delta-hedged position", "body": "The term \\((\\Delta S/S)^{2}\\) is the square of the return over the interval, and its expected value is \\(\\sigma_{\\text{real}}^{2}\\Delta t\\), where \\(\\sigma_{\\text{real}}\\) is the volatility that the share in fact exhibits. The expected gain over the interval is therefore \\(\\tfrac12\\Gamma S^{2}(\\sigma_{\\text{real}}^{2}-\\sigma_{\\text{imp}}^{2})\\Delta t\\), and the gain over the life of the option is the sum of such terms."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:14.9#33", "source_key": "fd-textbook:14.9", "chapter": 14, "section": "14.9", "heading": "★ The profit and loss of a delta-hedged position", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 33, "title": "Chapter 14, The Greeks and Dynamic Hedging — 14.9 ★ The profit and loss of a delta-hedged position", "body": "Three conclusions follow. A delta-hedged purchase of an option is profitable if the share proves more volatile than the volatility at which the option was bought, and unprofitable if it proves less volatile; for a delta-hedged sale the signs are reversed. The direction of the share price does not enter. And the gain depends on the path, because each interval is weighted by \\(\\Gamma S^{2}\\): volatility that occurs while the share price is near the strike and expiration is near counts for more than volatility that occurs elsewhere. For the dealer, on any day the breakeven move in the share price is that for which \\((\\Delta S/S)^{2}=\\sigma_{\\text{imp}}^{2}\\Delta t\\), namely \\(0.39/\\sqrt{252}=2.46\\) percent, or about $0.69 on a share price of $28. On days when the price moves by less, the dealer gains; on days when it moves by more, the dealer loses."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:14.10#34", "source_key": "fd-textbook:14.10", "chapter": 14, "section": "14.10", "heading": "Synthetic options and portfolio insurance", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 34, "title": "Chapter 14, The Greeks and Dynamic Hedging — 14.10 Synthetic options and portfolio insurance", "body": "Delta hedging can be read in the opposite direction. The dealer who buys \\(100{,}000\\times\\Delta\\) shares and adjusts the holding has created, by trading, a position whose value follows that of 100,000 purchased calls, and uses it to offset the calls that were written. An option that is produced by trading the underlying asset according to its delta is called a synthetic option."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:14.10#35", "source_key": "fd-textbook:14.10", "chapter": 14, "section": "14.10", "heading": "Synthetic options and portfolio insurance", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 35, "title": "Chapter 14, The Greeks and Dynamic Hedging — 14.10 Synthetic options and portfolio insurance", "body": "Section 13.2 described a fund with a portfolio of $12,000,000 that follows an index standing at 6,000, and that buys three-month puts with a strike of 5,700 to place a floor under its value. The fund may instead create the put synthetically. From Table 14.1 the delta of the put is \\(e^{-qT}[N(d_1)-1]=e^{-0.003}(0.7558-1)=-0.2435\\). The portfolio together with the put has the exposure of \\(1-0.2435=0.7565\\) of the portfolio. The fund obtains the same exposure by selling 24.35 percent of the portfolio, $2,922,000, and investing the proceeds at the risk-free rate. If the index falls to 5,700 after one week, the delta of the put becomes −0.4518, and the proportion sold must be raised to 45.18 percent. If the index rises, shares are bought back. In practice the adjustment is made by selling and buying index futures, which is cheaper than trading the shares."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:14.10#36", "source_key": "fd-textbook:14.10", "chapter": 14, "section": "14.10", "heading": "Synthetic options and portfolio insurance", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 36, "title": "Chapter 14, The Greeks and Dynamic Hedging — 14.10 Synthetic options and portfolio insurance", "body": "The procedure has the cost described in Section 14.3: the fund sells after prices have fallen and buys after they have risen. It also rests on two assumptions that fail when they are most needed. It assumes that the market moves by small steps, so that the fund can trade at each level on the way down, and it assumes that the fund's own sales do not move the price. A purchased put requires neither assumption. The difference is the subject of the historical note.\nConcept check 14.3\nA portfolio is delta neutral, with a gamma of −2,000 and a vega of −5,000 per percentage point. Which of the following reduces both exposures toward zero?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:14.10#37", "source_key": "fd-textbook:14.10", "chapter": 14, "section": "14.10", "heading": "Synthetic options and portfolio insurance", "kind": "notes", "ord": 37, "title": "Chapter 14, The Greeks and Dynamic Hedging — 14.10 Synthetic options and portfolio insurance", "body": "Historical note\nIn 1981 Hayne Leland and Mark Rubinstein, with John O'Brien, founded a firm to sell portfolio insurance of the kind described in Section 14.10 to pension funds. By the autumn of 1987 the strategy was applied to assets estimated at between $60 billion and $90 billion. In the week of 12 October 1987 the American stock market fell by about 10 percent, and the programs called for large sales of index futures. On Monday 19 October the Dow Jones Industrial Average fell by 22.6 percent, the largest fall in one day in its history. The commission appointed by the President to examine the event, chaired by Nicholas Brady, found that the sales of a small number of portfolio insurers accounted for a substantial part of the selling pressure in the futures market on that day. The insurers could not complete the sales that their programs required, and the floors that they had promised were not achieved."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:14.10#38", "source_key": "fd-textbook:14.10", "chapter": 14, "section": "14.10", "heading": "Synthetic options and portfolio insurance", "kind": "notes", "ord": 38, "title": "Chapter 14, The Greeks and Dynamic Hedging — 14.10 Synthetic options and portfolio insurance", "body": "The episode showed that a hedge which depends on trading in a falling market is not equivalent to an option that has been bought, and that a strategy which is sound for one investor may fail when many follow it together. It also changed the pricing of index options permanently, in a manner that Chapter 15 describes."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:14.summary#39", "source_key": "fd-textbook:14.summary", "chapter": 14, "section": "14.summary", "heading": "Summary", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 39, "title": "Chapter 14, The Greeks and Dynamic Hedging — 14.summary Summary", "body": "A written option cannot be hedged satisfactorily by a naked position, a covered position, or a stop-loss rule.\nDelta is the sensitivity of the price of an option to the price of the underlying: \\(N(d_1)\\) for a call and \\(N(d_1)-1\\) for a put on a share that pays no dividend. A position is delta neutral when its delta is zero.\nDelta changes with the share price and with time, so that a delta hedge must be rebalanced. With continuous rebalancing and volatility as assumed, the cost of hedging an option equals its Black–Scholes–Merton value.\nGamma is the sensitivity of delta to the price of the underlying. A delta-neutral position changes in value by about \\(\\tfrac12\\Gamma(\\Delta S)^{2}\\) when the price moves by \\(\\Delta S\\).\nTheta is the sensitivity to the passage of time. \\(\\Theta+rS\\Delta+\\tfrac12\\sigma^{2}S^{2}\\Gamma=r\\Pi\\): positive gamma is paid for by negative theta.\nVega is the sensitivity to volatility and rho the sensitivity to the interest rate. Gamma and vega can be changed only by trading options."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:14.summary#40", "source_key": "fd-textbook:14.summary", "chapter": 14, "section": "14.summary", "heading": "Summary", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 40, "title": "Chapter 14, The Greeks and Dynamic Hedging — 14.summary Summary", "body": "An option can be created synthetically by trading the underlying asset according to delta. The procedure fails when prices move discontinuously or when the trading itself moves the price."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:14.exercises#41", "source_key": "fd-textbook:14.exercises", "chapter": 14, "section": "14.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 41, "title": "Chapter 14, The Greeks and Dynamic Hedging — 14.exercises Exercises", "body": "14.1 A share that pays no dividend trades at $50 and has a volatility of 30 percent. The risk-free rate is 4 percent. A dealer has written six-month European calls on 20,000 shares with a strike of $50. Find the delta of one call and the delta hedge. (From Exercise 12.1, \\(d_1=0.2003\\).)\n\\(\\Delta=N(0.2003)=0.5794\\). The written calls have a delta of \\(-20{,}000\\times0.5794=-11{,}588\\). The dealer buys 11,588 shares, at a cost of \\(11{,}588\\times50=\\$579{,}400\\).\n14.2 A share that pays no dividend trades at $40 and has a volatility of 25 percent. The risk-free rate is 6 percent. An investor holds nine-month European puts on 10,000 shares with a strike of $45. Find the delta of one put and the trade in shares that makes the position delta neutral. (From Exercise 12.2, \\(d_1=-0.2279\\).)\n\\(N(d_1)=N(-0.2279)=0.4099\\), so that \\(\\Delta_{\\text{put}}=0.4099-1=-0.5901\\). The puts held have a delta of \\(10{,}000\\times(-0.5901)=-5{,}901\\). The investor buys 5,901 shares. The position is then a hedged put: it gains if the share price moves substantially in either direction."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:14.exercises#42", "source_key": "fd-textbook:14.exercises", "chapter": 14, "section": "14.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 42, "title": "Chapter 14, The Greeks and Dynamic Hedging — 14.exercises Exercises", "body": "14.3 For a one-year European call with a strike of $95 on a share at $100 with a volatility of 20 percent and a risk-free rate of 5 percent, \\(d_1=0.6065\\) and \\(d_2=0.4065\\). Find the gamma, the vega per percentage point, and the theta per calendar day.\n\\(N'(0.6065)=0.3989e^{-0.1839}=0.3319\\). \\(\\Gamma=0.3319/(100\\times0.20\\times1)=0.0166\\). \\(\\mathcal{V}=100\\times1\\times0.3319=33.19\\), or $0.332 per percentage point. \\(N(d_2)=0.6578\\) and \\(Ke^{-rT}=90.367\\). \\(\\Theta=-100\\times0.3319\\times0.20/2-0.05\\times90.367\\times0.6578=-3.319-2.972=-6.291\\) per year, or \\(-6.291/365=-\\$0.0172\\) per calendar day.\n14.4 A delta-neutral portfolio has a gamma of −4,000. Estimate the change in its value if the price of the underlying share moves at once (a) from $60 to $62 and (b) from $60 to $57.\nBy Result 14.2 with delta equal to zero and no passage of time, \\(\\Delta\\Pi\\approx\\tfrac12\\Gamma(\\Delta S)^{2}\\). (a) \\(\\tfrac12\\times(-4{,}000)\\times4=-\\$8{,}000\\). (b) \\(\\tfrac12\\times(-4{,}000)\\times9=-\\$18{,}000\\). The position loses in both cases; a move one and a half times as large produces a loss two and a quarter times as large."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:14.exercises#43", "source_key": "fd-textbook:14.exercises", "chapter": 14, "section": "14.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 43, "title": "Chapter 14, The Greeks and Dynamic Hedging — 14.exercises Exercises", "body": "14.5 A delta-neutral portfolio has a gamma of −3,000. A traded call has a delta of 0.62 and a gamma of 1.50. Find the trades that make the portfolio both gamma neutral and delta neutral.\n\\(w_T=-(-3{,}000)/1.50=2{,}000\\): buy 2,000 of the traded calls. They add a delta of \\(2{,}000\\times0.62=1{,}240\\), so that 1,240 shares are sold. The portfolio then has a delta of zero and a gamma of zero.\n14.6 A delta-neutral portfolio on a share priced at $80, with a volatility of 30 percent, is worth $200,000 and has a gamma of −500. The risk-free rate is 5 percent. Find its theta per year and per trading day, and state what the result means.\nBy Result 14.3, \\(\\Theta=r\\Pi-\\tfrac12\\sigma^{2}S^{2}\\Gamma=0.05\\times200{,}000-\\tfrac12\\times0.09\\times6{,}400\\times(-500)=10{,}000+144{,}000=\\$154{,}000\\) per year, or \\(154{,}000/252=\\$611\\) per trading day. If the share price does not move, the portfolio gains $611 in a day. This is the compensation for the losses that negative gamma produces when the price does move."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:14.exercises#44", "source_key": "fd-textbook:14.exercises", "chapter": 14, "section": "14.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 44, "title": "Chapter 14, The Greeks and Dynamic Hedging — 14.exercises Exercises", "body": "14.7 A delta-neutral portfolio has a gamma of −5,000 and a vega of −8,000 per percentage point. Option 1 has a delta of 0.60, a gamma of 0.50, and a vega of 2.00 per percentage point; option 2 has a delta of 0.50, a gamma of 0.80, and a vega of 1.20 per percentage point. Find the trades that make the portfolio neutral in delta, gamma, and vega.\nGamma: \\(0.50w_1+0.80w_2=5{,}000\\). Vega: \\(2.00w_1+1.20w_2=8{,}000\\). Multiplying the first by 4 and subtracting the second, \\(2.00w_2=12{,}000\\), so that \\(w_2=6{,}000\\) and \\(w_1=(5{,}000-4{,}800)/0.50=400\\). Buy 400 of option 1 and 6,000 of option 2. Their delta is \\(400\\times0.60+6{,}000\\times0.50=3{,}240\\); sell 3,240 shares.\n14.8 A pension fund holds a portfolio of $50,000,000 that follows a stock index. It wishes to create synthetically a six-month put on the portfolio, for which \\(N(d_1)=0.68\\); the dividend yield on the index is 2 percent. (a) What proportion of the portfolio should be sold at the outset? (b) Explain why the fund might prefer a purchased put, although the synthetic put has no initial cost."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:14.exercises#45", "source_key": "fd-textbook:14.exercises", "chapter": 14, "section": "14.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 45, "title": "Chapter 14, The Greeks and Dynamic Hedging — 14.exercises Exercises", "body": "(a) \\(\\Delta_{\\text{put}}=e^{-0.02\\times0.5}(0.68-1)=0.9900\\times(-0.32)=-0.3168\\). The fund sells 31.68 percent of the portfolio, $15,840,000, and invests the proceeds at the risk-free rate. (b) The synthetic put is not free: its cost is incurred through time, by selling after falls and buying after rises, and on average equals the value of the put. The cost is uncertain and is larger if the market proves more volatile than expected. The protection also depends on the ability to trade at each price as the market falls. If the market falls abruptly, or if many funds sell together, the floor is not achieved. A purchased put fixes both the cost and the floor in advance."}]$aim_json$::jsonb, 'Financial Derivatives (MacroNations textbook)');
